.TH V4-DISASM 1 "October 27, 2026" "UNIX Fourth Edition" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v4-disasm \- PDP-11 disassembler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v4-disasm
.RB [ \-a ] [ \-k ] [ \-n ] [ \-s ] [ \-v ] [ \-L\ \fIlabels\fR ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I v4-disasm
disassembles PDP-11 binaries in the PDP-11 \fBa.out\fR format. It attempts to 
distinguish between code and data by performing a recursive walk of the 
executable segments, starting from the entry point and following 
branch and jump instructions.
.PP
The program supports binaries with magic numbers \fB0407\fR and \fB0410\fR.
.PP
The following options may be used:
.TP
.B \-a
Print the octal address and the raw instruction bytes for every line of 
output.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep "implausible" instructions. By default, the recursive walk may 
reject certain instruction sequences that are unlikely to occur in 
standard code; \fB\-k\fR forces their inclusion.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not print padding information between the text and data segments.
.TP
.B \-s
Attempt to infer and print C-style function signatures. This is done by 
analyzing stack pointer offsets and register usage (e.g., \fBr0\fR) 
within the function body.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode. Prints statistics regarding label generation and the 
total number of bytes analyzed as code and data to the standard error 
stream.
.TP
.B \-L \fIlabels\fR
Set the budget for automatically generated labels. The disassembler 
will limit the number of synthetic labels (e.g., \fBL000100\fR) to this 
value to prevent output bloat.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B a.out
The input binary file. Must be a valid PDP-11 UNIX V4 object file.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBv4-as\fR(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
If the input file is too short to contain a header, the message 
\fI"too short"\fR is printed.
.PP
If the file does not match the expected PDP-11 UNIX V4 magic numbers, 
the message \fI"not a 0407/0410 a.out"\fR is printed.
.PP
If the image exceeds the addressable memory limit of the disassembler 
(64KB), the message \fI"image too large"\fR is printed.
.SH BUGS
speculative walking might misidentify data as code (or vice-versa) in binaries with 
highly unconventional control flows or dense jump tables (or by using \fBv4-cc\fR(1) peephole optimizer pass).